== 4. Landauer floor as a lower bound with an inefficiency multiplier beta = c/(kT per nat)
CT7 with price beta*kT per nat: D* = sqrt(2)*sqrt(beta)*sqrt(kT)*sigma/(2*sqrt(w)) ; J* = sqrt(2)*sqrt(beta)*sqrt(kT)*sigma*sqrt(w) ; ratio to floor = sqrt(beta)
alpha=1.5: J*(beta)/J*(1) vs beta^((a-1)/a), sensing share: [(1.0, np.float64(1.0), 1.0, np.float64(0.3333)), (10.0, np.float64(2.1544), 2.1544, np.float64(0.3333)), (1000000.0, np.float64(100.0), 100.0, np.float64(0.3333))]
alpha=2.0: J*(beta)/J*(1) vs beta^((a-1)/a), sensing share: [(1.0, np.float64(1.0), 1.0, np.float64(0.5)), (10.0, np.float64(3.1623), 3.1623, np.float64(0.5)), (1000000.0, np.float64(1000.0), 1000.0, np.float64(0.5))]
alpha=2.5: J*(beta)/J*(1) vs beta^((a-1)/a), sensing share: [(1.0, np.float64(1.0), 1.0, np.float64(0.6)), (10.0, np.float64(3.9811), 3.9811, np.float64(0.6)), (1000000.0, np.float64(3981.0717), 3981.0717, np.float64(0.6))]
=> J* is nondecreasing in the price per nat (envelope: dJ*/dc = reads/time > 0), so ANY actual price >= kT gives J* >= floor.
   The optimum's cost SHARES do not depend on beta; only the scales T0, J1 move (by beta^(1/a), beta^((a-1)/a)).
   beta itself is a measured, per-scale number (FRAMEWORK 3.11 beta_phys); the theory does not predict it.

